  "summary": "Three recent preprints show strain or gating reshaping graphene band structure to produce valley-selective, flat-band, and miniband states.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a common approach to controlling the low-energy electronic structure of graphene through external mechanical strain or electrostatic gating. In wrinkled graphene nanoribbons, tight-binding and quantum transport calculations show gate-tunable valley filtering and splitting. In multilayer graphene with rhombohedral stacking, strain generates giant pseudo-electric fields that produce flat bands, enhanced correlations, and topological boundary states. In Bernal bilayer graphene, a patterned graphite gate defines a superlattice whose miniband insulating states and band structure are electrostatically tunable. The new element is the use of strain-induced pseudo-fields and gate-defined superlattices as design parameters for valley and correlated states. The items do not establish a single mechanism across the three systems, so the shared thread is control of band structure rather than a unified physical picture.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
}
